The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (x2apic_mode) {
    __x2apic_enable();
} else {
    /* ... APICBASE programming without checking x2APIC state */
    rdmsr(MSR_IA32_APICBASE, l, h);
    l &= ~MSR_IA32_APICBASE_BASE;
    l |= MSR_IA32_APICBASE_ENABLE | mp_lapic_addr;
    wrmsr(MSR_IA32_APICBASE, l, h);
}
Reason: During resume, firmware may re-enable x2APIC mode (via MSR_IA32_APICBASE), but the kernel’s `x2apic_mode` variable is 0 because it was disabled earlier. The else branch directly programs the APICBASE register assuming xAPIC mode, which is invalid when hardware is in x2APIC mode. This mismatch causes hangs as the kernel uses xAPIC register accesses on hardware operating in x2APIC mode.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (x2apic_mode) {
    __x2apic_enable();
} else {
    if (x2apic_enabled()) {
        pr_warn_once("x2apic: re-enabled by firmware during resume. Disabling\n");
        __x2apic_disable();
    }
    /* ... existing APICBASE programming ... */
}
static inline void __x2apic_disable(void) { } // for !CONFIG_X86_X2APIC
Reason: The added check `if (x2apic_enabled())` detects whether hardware is in x2APIC mode despite the kernel expecting it to be disabled. If so, it explicitly disables x2APIC via `__x2apic_disable()` before proceeding with the xAPIC-based APICBASE programming. The stub for non-x2APIC configurations ensures compilation safety. This prevents the mismatch by ensuring the hardware is in the expected mode.

